## Solution

### (a) Sketches of the coverings

**Preliminary observation.** By the Seifert–van Kampen theorem (using open neighborhoods of the wedge point that deformation retract onto each summand), 
$$\pi_1(S^1 \vee S^2) \cong \pi_1(S^1) * \pi_1(S^2) \cong \mathbb{Z} * 0 \cong \mathbb{Z}.$$
Since $S^2$ is simply connected, the only "non-trivial" part of the fundamental group comes from the $S^1$ factor.

---

**Universal covering.** Since $\pi_1(S^1 \vee S^2) \cong \mathbb{Z}$, the universal cover is a $\mathbb{Z}$-fold (countably infinite) cover. It is constructed by "unwrapping" the $S^1$ into its universal cover $\mathbb{R}$, and at every lift of the wedge point (i.e., at every integer $n \in \mathbb{Z} \subset \mathbb{R}$), attaching a copy of $S^2$ (which is its own universal cover since it is simply connected).

> **Picture:** The real line $\mathbb{R}$ with a 2-sphere $S^2$ attached at each integer point $n \in \mathbb{Z}$.

The covering map $p: \widetilde{X} \to S^1 \vee S^2$ sends $\mathbb{R} \to S^1$ via the usual exponential $t \mapsto e^{2\pi i t}$, and each copy of $S^2$ maps homeomorphically onto the $S^2$ in the wedge.

---

**Connected 2-fold covering.** Connected 2-fold coverings correspond (up to equivalence) to index-2 subgroups of $\pi_1 \cong \mathbb{Z}$. The unique index-2 subgroup of $\mathbb{Z}$ is $2\mathbb{Z}$, so there is exactly **one** connected 2-fold covering. It is built from the 2-fold cover of $S^1$ (which is $S^1 \to S^1$, $z \mapsto z^2$); the wedge point has two preimages, and at each preimage we attach a copy of $S^2$ (since $S^2$ is simply connected, its 2-fold cover corresponding to the trivial subgroup restricted to the $S^2$ part is just $S^2$ itself mapped homeomorphically).

> **Picture:** A circle $S^1$ with two 2-spheres $S^2$ attached at two distinct points $p_1, p_2$ on the circle (the two preimages of the wedge point).

The covering map $q: \widehat{X} \to S^1 \vee S^2$ sends the covering circle to $S^1$ via $z \mapsto z^2$, and each of the two copies of $S^2$ maps homeomorphically onto the $S^2$ in the wedge.

---

### (b) Homology of the connected 2-fold covering

Let $\widehat{X}$ denote the connected 2-fold covering space described above:
$$\widehat{X} = S^1 \cup_{p_1} S^2_1 \cup_{p_2} S^2_2,$$
where $S^2_1$ is attached at $p_1 \in S^1$ and $S^2_2$ is attached at $p_2 \in S^1$, with $p_1 \neq p_2$.

We compute $H_*(\widehat{X})$ using the **Mayer–Vietoris sequence**. Let:
- $U$ = an open neighborhood of $S^1$ in $\widehat{X}$ that deformation retracts onto $S^1$,
- $V$ = the union of open neighborhoods of $S^2_1$ and $S^2_2$ that deformation retract onto $S^2_1 \sqcup S^2_2$.

Then $U \cap V$ is a disjoint union of two small open disks (neighborhoods of $p_1$ and $p_2$), hence $U \cap V \simeq \{*\} \sqcup \{*\}$ (two contractible components).

The relevant homology groups of the pieces are:

| Space | $H_0$ | $H_1$ | $H_2$ |
|---|---|---|---|
| $U \cap V$ | $\mathbb{Z}^2$ | $0$ | $0$ |
| $U$ | $\mathbb{Z}$ | $\mathbb{Z}$ | $0$ |
| $V$ | $\mathbb{Z}^2$ | $0$ | $\mathbb{Z}^2$ |

The Mayer–Vietoris sequence (with $\widehat{X} = U \cup V$) gives:

$$\cdots \to H_2(U \cap V) \to H_2(U) \oplus H_2(V) \to H_2(\widehat{X}) \to H_1(U \cap V) \to H_1(U) \oplus H_1(V) \to H_1(\widehat{X}) \to H_0(U \cap V) \to H_0(U) \oplus H_0(V) \to H_0(\widehat{X}) \to 0.$$

**Degree 2:** 
$$0 \to 0 \oplus \mathbb{Z}^2 \to H_2(\widehat{X}) \to 0,$$
so $H_2(\widehat{X}) \cong \mathbb{Z} \oplus \mathbb{Z}$.

**Degree 1:**
$$0 \to \mathbb{Z} \oplus 0 \to H_1(\widehat{X}) \to \ker\big[H_0(U \cap V) \to H_0(U) \oplus H_0(V)\big] \to 0.$$

The map $H_0(U \cap V) = \mathbb{Z}^2 \to H_0(U) \oplus H_0(V) = \mathbb{Z} \oplus \mathbb{Z}^2$ sends the two generators (corresponding to the two components of $U \cap V$) to $(1, 1, 0)$ and $(1, 0, 1)$ respectively. This map is injective, so its kernel is $0$. Therefore:
$$0 \to \mathbb{Z} \to H_1(\widehat{X}) \to 0,$$
giving $H_1(\widehat{X}) \cong \mathbb{Z}$.

**Degree 0:** $\widehat{X}$ is connected, so $H_0(\widehat{X}) \cong \mathbb{Z}$.

**Higher degrees:** Since $\widehat{X}$ is a 2-dimensional CW-complex, $H_n(\widehat{X}) = 0$ for all $n \geq 3$.

---

### Result

The homology of the connected 2-fold covering space of $S^1 \vee S^2$ is:

$$H_n(\widehat{X}) = \begin{cases} \mathbb{Z} & n = 0, \\ \mathbb{Z} & n = 1, \\ \mathbb{Z} \oplus \mathbb{Z} & n = 2, \\ 0 & n \geq 3. \end{cases}$$

**Intuition / sanity check.** The 2-fold cover "doubles" the $S^1$ into a single $S^1$ (wrapping twice), which still has $H_1 = \mathbb{Z}$. The $S^2$ factor, being simply connected, lifts to two separate copies of $S^2$ (one at each preimage of the wedge point), giving $H_2 = \mathbb{Z}^2$. This is consistent with the general principle that for a covering $p: \widehat{X} \to X$ of degree $d$, the Euler characteristics satisfy $\chi(\widehat{X}) = d \cdot \chi(X)$. Here $\chi(S^1 \vee S^2) = \chi(S^1) + \chi(S^2) - 1 = 0 + 2 - 1 = 1$, and $\chi(\widehat{X}) = 2 \cdot 1 = 2 = 1 - 1 + 2$. ✓

